A||||10||fiero56||winter storage, tires and concrete||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||12-31-2001||03:59 AM||||I parked my Formula about 2 months ago for the winter. I put new tires on it just before I parked it in the garage for the winter, and I got to thinking today wondering if I should put a piece of wood between the tires on the bare concrete. I have heard negatives of extended storage of tires on concrete, but can't remember what the problem is. Good idea or bad?<P>BTW: I just got back from a trip to Oregon for for the past week and a half. It was in the 40s and 50s all week there, I get back and its snowing! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||12-31-2001||07:02 AM||||I do know its def a nono to leave it stored with a flat.......lisa....... The best way, if your gonna store it (I dont believe in it myself) is use jack stands to get them off the ground, BUT keep the suspension loaded. Shocks/struts will go bad stored extended. Otherwise, tires may get flat spots that will never go away. So you can buy all new tires all over again.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87ECOsomething||12-31-2001||08:09 AM||||Drew - Gus kept that '76 Corvette on wood for the three years it sat in his garage.  He said someone suggested doing that to slow down the dry-rotting process.<P>$0.02 ...<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fiero56:<BR><B>..., I get back and its snowing!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Welcome home!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by 87ECOsomething (edited 12-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||CoolBlue87GT||12-31-2001||09:56 AM||||I've kept mine stored on concrete floor all it's life.<P>So far so good. To prevent dry rot, I've made sure all of the tire had a good coat of "Black Magic" tire wet, Tread, back & front sides. <P>- Be careful if you do the tread, as first few miles would be like driving on snow.<P>I've moved the car a few feet forward each month, and back to prevent flat spotting. (1/4 at a time)<P>Tire pressure will drop a few lbs from sitting. - Has never been much of a problem.<P>As far as putting on jack stands, I've thought about that, never tried it. Would like to hear more about that.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/CoolBluesig.gif"> ||||reg||6
Z||000004||RossT||12-31-2001||10:32 AM||||You can put it on car dollies to avoid flat spots.  <P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image002.jpg">   <P>Also makes it easy to move your car aroung with one hand!!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image007.jpg"> <P>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 12-31-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 12-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||KissMySSFiero||12-31-2001||10:41 AM||||where do you get those dollies and how much are they?  If your worried about flat spots on the tires,  go to a junk yard and get a set of wheels.  you should be able to find some for about $50 or even less with tires.  they dont have to be great tires unless you plan on driving on them.  <P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <BR>Two 88FORMULAS Red & Yellow<BR>        <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>         <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||RossT||12-31-2001||12:38 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.harborfreight.com" TARGET=_blank>www.harborfreight.com</A>  sometimes has them on sale for $99 / set of 4.  They also have free shipping, and you need it because these are very very heavy and well made. Other companies make car dollies (for much more) but these by far are the best for the money.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tabs31||12-31-2001||01:06 PM||||I love that dollie idea. Next winter I'm gonna look into that<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tabs31_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||12-31-2001||06:03 PM||||Best way is on jack stands, or tires you don't care about.  I hear a thick square of shag carpet (maybe two or three) will prevent most flat-spotting as well.<P>Good idea to start any car at least once a month (once a WEEK is better!!!!!!) to keep the juices flowing.  moving the tires 1/4th a rev at that time is good too!<P>Just remenber to hook up all 4 exhaust gas hoses!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">      <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red.                     "Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver, In-Progreess.                                        -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||CoolBlue87GT||12-31-2001||10:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RossT:<BR><B> these by far are the best for the money.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Looks neat.<BR>How do you get the car on/off them ?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000010||Haze_Performance||12-31-2001||10:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by CoolBlue87GT:<BR><B> Looks neat.<BR>How do you get the car on/off them ?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>You just jack up the car & slide them under.. <BR><P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/goodlife.gif">         <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Pace Car |FOR SALE|||65.230.197.225||reg||14
